A projection onto the y-axis in 2D space maps any vector to its y-component. To find the matrix that represents this projection, consider how the standard basis vectors are transformed. 

1. The standard basis vectors in 2D are $ \mathbf{e}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} $ and $ \mathbf{e}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} $.
2. Projecting $ \mathbf{e}_1 $ onto the y-axis: $ P\mathbf{e}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} $, since it lies on the x-axis.
3. Projecting $ \mathbf{e}_2 $ onto the y-axis: $ P\mathbf{e}_2 = \begin{bbfmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} $, as it already lies on the y-axis.
4. The projection matrix is formed by placing the images of $ \mathbf{e}_1 $ and $ \mathbf{e}_2 $ as columns in a matrix, which gives: $ P = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix} $.

Thus, the correct matrix is $ \boxed{\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}} $.
\boxed{\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}}
The matrix that corresponds to projecting onto the $ y $-axis in 2D space is found by determining how the standard basis vectors are transformed. The standard basis vectors are $ \mathbf{e}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} $ and $ \mathbf{e}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} $.

- Projecting $ \mathbf{e}_1 $ onto the $ y $-axis results in $ \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} $ because it lies on the $ x $-axis.
- Projecting $ \mathbf{e}_2 $ onto the $ y $-axis results in $ \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} $ because it already lies on the $ y $-axis.

Placing these transformed vectors as columns in a matrix gives the projection matrix $ P = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix} $.

